{"question":"In published pre-2020 coronavirus vaccine comparisons, do S-, S1-, and RBD-based candidates show reproducible relationships among immune responses, neutralization, and protection across relevant animal models?","goal":"Clarifying which spike-focused immune measures track protection could guide research on prevention while avoiding claims that any SARS-CoV-2 vaccine is proven.","evidence":"Comparative published studies reporting antigen design, safety observations, immune responses, neutralization, and protection in coronavirus animal models; human evidence should be assessed separately.","outcomes":["Across studies, higher neutralization consistently coincides with reduced infection or disease, supporting a transferable correlate.","Protection varies despite similar neutralization or cellular responses, arguing that additional mechanisms or model-specific factors must be considered."]}
{"question":"Do published coronavirus vaccine studies show that RBD-focused designs improve efficacy without introducing distinct safety concerns compared with full-length S or S1 designs?","goal":"A clearer safety–efficacy comparison could guide vaccine-prevention research without implying that any candidate is safe or effective in humans.","evidence":"Published comparative preclinical and, where available by the cutoff, clinical literature reporting construct-specific safety, breadth, neutralization, cellular responses, and protection outcomes.","outcomes":["RBD-focused candidates repeatedly show comparable or better protection with fewer safety concerns.","RBD-focused candidates show narrower or less durable protection, or safety advantages are not reproducible."]}
{"question":"Across published coronavirus vaccine studies available by the cutoff, do spike-, S1-, or RBD-based candidates show reproducible associations between immune responses, neutralization, and protection?","goal":"Answering this would help prioritize prevention research while avoiding the unsupported conclusion that a SARS-CoV-2 vaccine is proven effective.","evidence":"Comparative published preclinical and, where available by the cutoff, clinical studies reporting antigen design, immune responses, neutralization, safety observations, and protection.","outcomes":["Higher neutralization or defined cellular responses repeatedly coincide with reduced infection or disease across models.","Protection varies despite similar measured immune responses, indicating important model-, assay-, or mechanism-specific factors."]}
{"question":"Do published coronavirus vaccine studies show that mucosal immunity, standardized immune assays, and improved animal models materially change interpretation of vaccine protection and safety?","goal":"Clarifying these methodological gaps would improve interpretation of prevention evidence and inform research on human disease interaction without claiming any candidate is proven.","evidence":"Literature comparisons linking assay methods, mucosal or systemic immune measures, animal-model characteristics, and reported protection or safety outcomes.","outcomes":["Protection estimates differ materially by model, mucosal assessment, or assay standardization.","Protection and safety rankings remain consistent across these methodological differences."]}
{"question":"Across published coronavirus comparisons available by the cutoff, do receptor usage and evolutionary relationships predict differences in human transmission, tissue tropism, or pathogenesis relevant to SARS-CoV-2?","goal":"Resolving this could guide research on SARS-CoV-2 human interaction and prevention while avoiding unsupported claims about disease mechanisms or treatment targets.","evidence":"Published comparative studies reporting coronavirus receptor usage, phylogenetic relationships, host range, tissue tropism, transmission, and pathogenesis, with SARS-CoV-2 findings assessed separately from SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV analogies.","outcomes":["Shared receptor usage and related evolutionary features consistently associate with comparable host range, tissue tropism, or transmission across coronaviruses.","Coronaviruses with similar receptor usage show substantially different human transmission or pathogenesis, indicating that receptor usage is insufficient as a standalone predictor."]}

{"question":"Compare published SARS-CoV-2 spike-vaccine studies with the MERS spike-vaccine evidence framework, focusing on safety, antibody and cellular responses, neutralization, and protection.","goal":"It advances prevention research by organizing evidence relevant to vaccine design and evaluation without claiming that any vaccine is proven.","evidence":"Full published SARS-CoV-2 vaccine studies and comparative reviews reporting study design, populations or models, safety, immune responses, neutralization, and protection outcomes.","outcomes":["Consistent association between spike-directed neutralization and reduced infection or disease across independent SARS-CoV-2 studies would support transferability.","Protection without strong neutralization, or frequent safety/escape concerns despite spike-directed responses, would weaken a simple spike-only interpretation."]}
{"question":"Compare published SARS-CoV-2 receptor-usage and spillover studies with the SARS/MERS evolutionary literature to determine whether receptor interaction plausibly explains human tropism and disease-relevant transmission.","goal":"It advances understanding of human infection and prevention by identifying host-entry mechanisms that could guide risk assessment and intervention research, without asserting a treatment.","evidence":"Published comparative receptor-binding, human-cell tropism, clinical, and evolutionary studies, with explicit separation of observational findings from mechanistic inference.","outcomes":["Independent studies linking receptor compatibility with SARS-CoV-2 entry across relevant human tissues would support a central receptor role.","Weak or inconsistent links after accounting for other entry and host factors would support a multifactorial explanation."]}
{"question":"Compare published SARS-CoV-2 vaccine studies available by the cutoff with the MERS review’s evaluation framework, including safety, antibody and cellular responses, neutralization, mucosal immunity, and protection.","goal":"It advances prevention research by organizing evidence relevant to vaccine evaluation without claiming that any vaccine is proven.","evidence":"Published SARS-CoV-2 vaccine reports or reviews describing study populations or models, safety, immune responses, neutralization, mucosal findings, and protection outcomes.","outcomes":["Consistent association between vaccine-induced neutralization and reduced SARS-CoV-2 infection or disease across independent studies would support transferability.","Protection that is weak, dissociated from neutralization, or limited by safety or mucosal-immunity concerns would weaken a simple spike-focused interpretation."]}
{"question":"Compare published SARS-CoV-2 receptor-usage, tissue-tropism, and evolutionary studies with coronavirus literature to assess whether receptor compatibility explains human infection or whether multiple host and viral factors are required.","goal":"It advances understanding of human disease and prevention by clarifying entry mechanisms without asserting a treatment or intervention.","evidence":"Published comparative studies reporting receptor binding or usage, relevant human-cell or tissue tropism, evolutionary comparisons, and clinical associations, with observations separated from mechanistic inference.","outcomes":["Independent studies linking receptor compatibility with SARS-CoV-2 entry across relevant human tissues would support a central receptor role.","Weak or inconsistent associations after accounting for other entry and host factors would support a multifactorial explanation."]}

{"question":"Across published comparative studies available by the cutoff, do SARS-CoV-2 and related SARS-like coronaviruses show consistent ACE2 usage and entry characteristics, and do those characteristics associate with human-relevant tissue tropism or transmission?","goal":"Answering this would improve understanding of human infection biology and help prioritize prevention or treatment research without claiming that receptor usage alone proves clinical relevance.","evidence":"Published SARS-CoV-2, SARS-CoV, MERS-CoV, and relevant bat-coronavirus studies reporting receptor usage, entry, tissue distribution, host range, transmission, and clinical or pathological observations, with in-vitro, animal, and human evidence kept distinct.","outcomes":["ACE2 usage and entry features are consistently demonstrated for SARS-CoV-2 and related viruses and correlate with human-relevant tissue or transmission observations.","Similar receptor usage occurs despite substantially different tissue tropism, transmission, or disease outcomes, indicating that receptor usage is not a sufficient standalone predictor."]}
{"question":"Do comparative wildlife-coronavirus studies show that receptor usage, phylogenetic relatedness, and broad species tropism reliably identify viruses with elevated spillover or pandemic risk?","goal":"Clarifying the predictive value of these indicators could guide surveillance and prevention priorities while avoiding unsupported claims about imminent spillover or human disease.","evidence":"Published comparative surveillance and spillover literature linking viral phylogeny, receptor usage, experimental host range, ecological exposure, documented cross-species infection, and subsequent human transmission.","outcomes":["Viruses with human-receptor usage and broad experimental tropism show higher documented spillover or sustained transmission across comparative datasets.","Receptor usage and experimental tropism frequently occur without human emergence, demonstrating substantial limits to these indicators."]}
{"question":"Do published direct SARS-CoV-2 studies confirm ACE2-dependent entry and human-relevant tissue distribution, and how do those findings compare with SHC014 and SARS-CoV observations?","goal":"This comparison would clarify mechanisms relevant to COVID-19 transmission and pathogenesis, helping prioritize prevention and treatment research without claiming that any target or intervention is proven.","evidence":"Published SARS-CoV-2 and comparative coronavirus studies reporting receptor dependence, primary human airway or tissue observations, animal or pathological findings, and clear separation of direct observations from engineered-virus inference.","outcomes":["Direct SARS-CoV-2 studies consistently link ACE2 dependence with infection of human-relevant tissues and compatible disease observations.","ACE2 dependence is observed but tissue distribution or disease outcomes vary substantially, indicating that receptor usage alone is insufficient."]}
{"question":"Across published SARS-like coronavirus studies, how often do spike-directed antibodies or vaccines cross-neutralize heterologous spikes, and what antigenic or structural features distinguish breadth from failure?","goal":"Resolving breadth and escape patterns could guide vaccine antigen selection and therapeutic antibody research while avoiding unsupported claims of protection.","evidence":"Published comparative studies reporting spike antigen design, antibody binding and neutralization across heterologous viruses, vaccine-induced responses, and protection outcomes, with experimental systems identified.","outcomes":["Cross-neutralization and protection remain robust across diverse SARS-like spikes.","Neutralization or protection drops sharply against antigenically distinct spikes, with reproducible features predicting failure."]}
{"question":"Do published SARS-CoV-2 studies distinguish gastrointestinal viral RNA or antigen detection from replication-competent virus and demonstrate whether gastrointestinal involvement contributes to transmission?","goal":"Resolving this would improve understanding of human disease interaction and transmission, informing prevention priorities without claiming that any route or intervention is proven.","evidence":"Published clinical, tissue, and virological comparisons reporting infectious-virus recovery or validated replication markers, timing relative to respiratory shedding, and epidemiological evidence for or against gastrointestinal transmission; keep RNA, antigen, and infectious-virus observations distinct.","outcomes":["Independent studies recover replication-competent virus from gastrointestinal specimens and associate it with transmission events.","Gastrointestinal RNA/antigen is common but infectious-virus recovery and transmission evidence are consistently absent or rare."]}
{"question":"Across direct SARS-CoV-2 studies, does ACE2-associated entry consistently correspond to infection of human airway and gastrointestinal tissues, or do tissue-specific host factors explain substantial differences?","goal":"This would refine mechanistic understanding of SARS-CoV-2 human interaction and help prioritize prevention and treatment research without asserting that ACE2 alone determines disease.","evidence":"Published SARS-CoV-2 studies comparing receptor expression, entry dependence, primary human airway and gastrointestinal tissue infection, and pathological or clinical observations, with cell-line, ex-vivo, animal, and human evidence separated.","outcomes":["ACE2-dependent entry and tissue infection are reproducible across airway and gastrointestinal models and align with human observations.","ACE2 is present but infection varies substantially by tissue context, indicating that receptor compatibility is insufficient as a standalone predictor."]}

{"question":"Compare published SARS-CoV-2 vaccine and antibody studies with the reported failure of SARS-based approaches against a novel-spike SARS-like virus, focusing on antigenic breadth, neutralization, safety, and protection endpoints.","goal":"This advances prevention research by identifying which immune endpoints and antigen designs are associated with protection against human disease, without claiming that any vaccine or antibody is proven.","evidence":"Published SARS-CoV-2 vaccine, antibody, and comparative coronavirus studies reporting antigen design, binding and neutralization, safety, cellular or mucosal responses, and infection or disease protection in humans or validated models.","outcomes":["Independent SARS-CoV-2 studies showing that breadth of spike recognition and neutralization tracks with reduced infection or disease would support robust spike-focused prevention.","Frequent discordance between neutralization and protection, or protection loss against antigenically distinct variants, would support a broader or more context-dependent immune explanation."]}
{"question":"Compare published SARS-CoV-2 receptor-entry and tissue-tropism studies with the SHC014/ACE2 observations to assess whether receptor compatibility predicts human airway infection and disease after accounting for other entry and host factors.","goal":"This advances understanding of human infection and prevention by clarifying entry determinants that may identify intervention priorities, without asserting a treatment.","evidence":"Published comparative studies reporting SARS-CoV-2 receptor binding or usage, entry in relevant human cells or tissues, tissue distribution, and associations with disease or transmission, with explicit separation of observation from mechanistic inference.","outcomes":["Consistent SARS-CoV-2 evidence linking receptor compatibility with entry across relevant human airway tissues, while controlling for other entry factors, would support a central receptor role.","Weak or inconsistent receptor-disease associations after accounting for tissue distribution and other host factors would support a multifactorial explanation."]}
{"question":"Compare published SARS-CoV-2 vaccine and antibody studies with the supplied MERS cross-reactivity findings, emphasizing breadth across isolates, neutralization, safety, cellular or mucosal responses, and infection or disease protection.","goal":"This advances prevention research by identifying which immune endpoints and antigen designs are associated with breadth and protection, without claiming any vaccine or antibody is proven.","evidence":"Published SARS-CoV-2 studies reporting antigen design, isolate panels, binding and neutralization, safety, cellular or mucosal immunity, and protection outcomes in humans or validated models.","outcomes":["Consistent breadth of neutralization across SARS-CoV-2 isolates together with reduced infection or disease would support a transferable spike-focused strategy.","Marked isolate-specific neutralization, escape, or protection that depends on non-neutralizing, cellular, or mucosal mechanisms would support a broader or context-dependent strategy."]}
{"question":"Compare published SARS-CoV-2 receptor-entry and tissue-tropism studies with comparative coronavirus antigenic and serologic evidence to assess whether receptor compatibility predicts human infection independently of other host and viral factors.","goal":"This advances understanding of human infection and prevention by clarifying which entry and host factors are plausible intervention priorities, without asserting a treatment.","evidence":"Published comparative studies reporting SARS-CoV-2 receptor binding or usage, entry in relevant human cells or tissues, tissue distribution, and associations with disease or transmission, with controls for other entry and host factors.","outcomes":["Consistent associations between receptor compatibility and entry across relevant human airway tissues, after accounting for other entry factors, would support a central receptor role.","Weak or inconsistent receptor-disease associations after accounting for tissue distribution, immunity, and other host factors would support a multifactorial explanation."]}
{"question":"Compare published SARS-CoV-2 vaccine and antibody studies with the supplied cross-virus breadth observations, assessing isolate breadth, neutralization, cellular or mucosal responses, safety, and infection or disease protection.","goal":"This advances prevention research by identifying immune endpoints associated with breadth and protection, without claiming that any vaccine or antibody is proven.","evidence":"Published SARS-CoV-2 studies reporting antigen design, isolate panels, binding and neutralization, safety, cellular or mucosal immunity, and protection outcomes in humans or validated models.","outcomes":["Breadth of neutralization consistently associated with reduced SARS-CoV-2 infection or disease would support a transferable breadth-focused strategy.","Frequent discordance between binding or neutralization breadth and protection, or protection limited by safety or mucosal findings, would support a more context-dependent strategy."]}
{"question":"Compare published SARS-CoV-2 studies of NSP16 or related conserved coronavirus functions with the MERS NSP16 findings, focusing on interferon antagonism, replication, disease associations, antiviral relevance, and protection outcomes.","goal":"This advances understanding of viral–human interaction and may prioritize prevention or treatment research while avoiding claims that a conserved target is clinically effective.","evidence":"Published SARS-CoV-2 comparative studies reporting NSP16 sequence/function, interferon or IFIT responses, replication, relevant human-cell or tissue findings, clinical associations, and any safety or protection evidence.","outcomes":["Independent SARS-CoV-2 studies linking NSP16 activity to interferon resistance and disease-related phenotypes would support conserved-function relevance.","Weak, inconsistent, or context-dependent associations after accounting for host and tissue factors would support a multifactorial explanation and weaken direct transfer from MERS."]}

{"question":"Do published clinical and virological studies distinguish gastrointestinal SARS-CoV-2 RNA or antigen from replication-competent virus and establish any transmission contribution?","goal":"Resolving this would refine understanding of human disease interaction and prevention priorities without treating a transmission route or intervention as proven.","evidence":"Published comparisons of infectious-virus recovery or validated replication markers, timing relative to respiratory shedding, and epidemiological transmission evidence.","outcomes":["Independent studies recover replication-competent virus from gastrointestinal specimens and associate it with transmission events.","Gastrointestinal RNA or antigen is common but infectious-virus recovery and transmission evidence are consistently absent or rare."]}
{"question":"Do direct SARS-CoV-2 studies establish ACE2-dependent entry and tissue distribution in human-relevant airway and gastrointestinal models, and do these findings align with clinical observations?","goal":"This would clarify mechanisms of human infection and help prioritize treatment and prevention research without claiming that ACE2 targeting or any vaccine is proven.","evidence":"Published SARS-CoV-2 studies comparing receptor dependence, primary human airway or gastrointestinal tissue infection, and pathological or clinical observations, with cell-line, ex-vivo, animal, and human evidence separated.","outcomes":["ACE2-dependent entry and tissue infection reproduce across airway and gastrointestinal models and align with human observations.","ACE2 is present or usable, but infection varies substantially by tissue context, showing receptor compatibility is insufficient alone."]}
{"question":"Do published comparative coronavirus studies identify reproducible host-range determinants that separate animal spillover from sustained human transmission?","goal":"This advances understanding of cross-species interaction and preparedness while avoiding the claim that animal outbreak evidence proves human transmission or any intervention.","evidence":"Published comparative studies of SADS-CoV, SARS-CoV, SARS-CoV-2, and related coronaviruses reporting host species, receptor usage, tissue tropism, transmission observations, and clearly separated in-vitro, animal, and human evidence.","outcomes":["Receptor and entry phenotypes consistently predict host range across multiple coronavirus lineages.","Host range varies despite similar receptor compatibility, implicating tissue, immune, ecological, or additional viral determinants."]}
{"question":"Do published SARS-CoV-2 studies demonstrate replication-competent gastrointestinal virus and a measurable contribution of gastrointestinal shedding to transmission?","goal":"Resolving this would refine transmission understanding and prevention priorities without treating fecal-oral transmission or any countermeasure as proven.","evidence":"Published clinical and virological studies reporting infectious-virus recovery or validated replication markers, timing relative to respiratory shedding, and epidemiological linkage to transmission; retain RNA, antigen, and infectious-virus results as separate evidence classes.","outcomes":["Independent studies recover replication-competent virus from gastrointestinal specimens and associate it with transmission events.","Gastrointestinal RNA or antigen is detected, but infectious-virus recovery and epidemiological transmission evidence remain consistently absent or rare."]}
{"question":"Do published SARS-CoV-2 studies distinguish gastrointestinal RNA or antigen from replication-competent virus and associate gastrointestinal findings with documented transmission using integrated virological, genomic, and epidemiological evidence?","goal":"Answering this would refine understanding of human infection and transmission and improve prevention prioritization without claiming any transmission route or intervention is proven.","evidence":"Published clinical and virological comparisons reporting infectious-virus recovery or validated replication markers, timing relative to respiratory shedding, contact-tracing findings, and genomic/phylogenetic analysis integrated with epidemiological timing.","outcomes":["Independent studies recover replication-competent virus from gastrointestinal specimens and link it, with compatible timing and epidemiology, to transmission events.","Gastrointestinal RNA or antigen is detected but infectious-virus recovery and integrated epidemiological/genomic evidence for transmission remain absent or rare."]}
{"question":"Do comparative wildlife-virus studies show that broader genomic surveillance and improved screening identify previously missed spillover, while still distinguishing viral detection from confirmed interspecies transmission?","goal":"This advances pandemic preparedness by improving interpretation of host-range and spillover signals relevant to prevention, without claiming that animal-virus detection establishes human disease or intervention efficacy.","evidence":"Published comparative surveillance studies using multiple assays and genomic approaches, with human sampling, exposure data, sequence linkage, and explicit separation of detection, suspected spillover, and confirmed transmission.","outcomes":["Broader screening substantially increases detection of divergent viruses and identifies epidemiologically supported cross-species transmission.","Broader screening increases diversity estimates but still finds little or no confirmed human transmission absent compatible sequence and epidemiological links."]}

{"question":"Compare published SARS-CoV-2 studies with the supplied MERS-CoV fusion findings to determine whether ion-dependent fusion mechanisms and fusion-peptide features are associated with SARS-CoV-2 entry, replication, or disease-related phenotypes.","goal":"This advances understanding of how SARS-CoV-2 interacts with human cells and may clarify prevention or treatment priorities without claiming that any fusion-related intervention is proven.","evidence":"Published comparative studies reporting SARS-CoV-2 spike or fusion-peptide sequence/function, receptor and protease controls, entry or fusion in relevant human airway cells or tissues, authentic-virus or validated-model replication, and associations with clinical severity or transmission.","outcomes":["Independent SARS-CoV-2 studies reproducing ion-dependent fusion effects across relevant human airway systems, with effects persisting after receptor and protease controls, would support a conserved co","factor-mediated entry mechanism.","Weak, cell-type-specific, or non-reproducible effects—or lack of association with replication or disease after adjustment for other entry factors—would support a predominantly multifactorial or assay-"]}
{"question":"Compare published SARS-CoV-2 functional entry studies with this healthy-tissue expression study to determine whether ACE2, TMPRSS2, CD147, or GRP78 is required or sufficient for entry in relevant human airway systems, and whether infection-associated ACE2 regulation is supported.","goal":"This advances understanding of SARS-CoV-2 interaction with human respiratory tissue and identifies evidence-supported host factors for prevention or treatment research, without claiming a treatment or vaccine is proven.","evidence":"Published studies using authentic SARS-CoV-2 or validated human-relevant entry systems, reporting receptor perturbation or rescue, receptor localization and abundance, protease context, infection or entry endpoints, and comparisons across airway cell types or tissue states.","outcomes":["Consistent loss or reduction of SARS-CoV-2 entry after ACE2 disruption, with reproducible modulation by TMPRSS2 and limited independent effects of CD147 or GRP78, would support ACE2-centered multif].","Weak ACE2 dependence in some relevant airway contexts, reproducible effects of candidate alternate-receptor perturbation, or infection-associated ACE2 regulation linked to entry would support a more","priority multifactorial model."]}
{"question":"Compare published SARS-CoV-2 functional-entry studies with the supplied abstract to determine the relative contributions of ACE2, host protease processing, and proposed alternative receptors in relevant human airway contexts.","goal":"This advances understanding of how SARS-CoV-2 interacts with human cells and distinguishes plausible host-factor mechanisms from assay-specific observations, without claiming that any host factor is a proven treatment or prevention target.","evidence":"Published studies using authentic SARS-CoV-2 or validated human-relevant systems, reporting receptor perturbation or rescue, protease context, receptor localization, entry or infection endpoints, and comparisons across airway cell types or tissue states.","outcomes":["Consistent ACE2-dependent entry across relevant airway systems, with protease modulation but little independent effect of candidate alternative receptors, would support an ACE2-centered model.","Reproducible ACE2-independent or alternative-receptor-associated entry under defined human-airway conditions, or strong tissue-state dependence after protease adjustment, would support a more context-","limitations_note"]}
{"question":"Compare published SARS-CoV-2 vaccine and antiviral studies with computationally inferred conserved regions and related-coronavirus evidence to test whether predicted conservation corresponds to immune breadth, antiviral activity, safety, or protection.","goal":"This advances treatment and prevention research by identifying which computationally prioritized features have experimentally supported relevance to SARS-CoV-2 immunity or viral control, without claiming a vaccine or antiviral is proven.","evidence":"Published SARS-CoV-2 studies reporting the predicted region or function, experimental immune breadth or antiviral endpoints, relevant controls, safety, and—where available—human or validated-model protection outcomes.","outcomes":["Independent SARS-CoV-2 studies linking conserved-region targeting to cross-isolate activity and reduced infection or disease would support prioritizing that feature.","Frequent discordance between predicted conservation and immune breadth, antiviral activity, or protection would support treating computational conservation as hypothesis-generating rather than outcome","limitations_note"]}

{"question":"Do published SARS-CoV-2 studies distinguish gastrointestinal RNA or antigen from replication-competent virus and link gastrointestinal findings to documented transmission?","goal":"It advances understanding of human infection and prevention without treating a transmission route or intervention as proven.","evidence":"Published studies separating RNA, antigen, replication markers, and infectious-virus recovery, with sampling timing, contact-tracing data, and genomic linkage where available.","outcomes":["Replication-competent virus is recovered from gastrointestinal specimens and compatible epidemiological/genomic links identify transmission.","RNA or antigen is detected but infectious-virus recovery and integrated transmission evidence are absent or rare."]}
{"question":"Do comparative wildlife-virus surveillance studies show that broader genomic screening identifies previously missed viruses while distinguishing detection from confirmed spillover?","goal":"It improves pandemic-preparedness interpretation and surveillance prioritization without claiming that wildlife detection proves human disease or intervention efficacy.","evidence":"Published comparisons of assay breadth, metagenomic or genomic detection, human sampling, exposure data, sequence linkage, and explicit classifications of detection versus suspected or confirmed transmission.","outcomes":["Broader assays substantially increase detection and identify epidemiologically supported cross-species transmission.","Broader assays increase diversity estimates but confirmed transmission remains absent or rare without compatible sequence and epidemiological evidence."]}
{"question":"Do SARS-CoV-2 serosurveys available by the cutoff show that screening-antibody reactivity is reliably confirmed by orthogonal neutralisation or other independent assays across low- and high-prevalence settings?","goal":"This advances understanding of human interaction with SARS-CoV-2 and improves interpretation of evidence used to guide prevention, without treating seropositivity as proven protection or a vaccine result.","evidence":"Published SARS-CoV-2 serosurveys or assay-validation comparisons reporting sampling frame, prevalence context, assay thresholds, orthogonal neutralisation or confirmatory results, and uncertainty intervals.","outcomes":["Independent confirmation rates remain high across settings and thresholds.","Confirmation rates vary substantially with prevalence, assay threshold, or population, indicating screening reactivity is not equivalent to infection."]}
{"question":"Do comparative coronavirus studies show that serologically detected infection can occur without severe lower-respiratory disease, and which virological or epidemiological measurements distinguish infection from severe disease and onward transmission?","goal":"This advances understanding of disease mechanisms and transmission patterns that inform prevention and treatment prioritization, without claiming that any intervention is effective.","evidence":"Published comparative studies reporting serology, respiratory disease severity, longitudinal clinical follow-up, viral detection or culture where available, host-risk factors, and contact-tracing or transmission outcomes.","outcomes":["Across studies, serologically detected mild infection is common and can be separated from severe disease and transmission using longitudinal evidence.","Serology closely tracks severe disease or transmission after accounting for sampling and assay limitations, weakening the analogy."]}
{"question":"Do independent published studies show that ACE2-based decoy molecules inhibit authentic SARS-CoV-2 infection in human-relevant systems and associate that activity with reduced disease or transmission?","goal":"This advances understanding of SARS-CoV-2 human interaction and candidate countermeasures without treating in-vitro inhibition as proven clinical benefit.","evidence":"Published independent studies comparing authentic-virus results with pseudovirus and cell-entry assays, using relevant human airway or clinical material where available, plus pharmacodynamic and clinical or epidemiological outcomes if reported.","outcomes":["Authentic-virus inhibition is independently reproduced and aligns with airway-model or clinical protection.","Activity is confined to entry surrogates or fails to translate under authentic-virus or human-relevant conditions."]}
{"question":"Do published translational studies resolve the exposure, persistence, distribution, and safety constraints of ACE2-Fc decoys, particularly possible cardiovascular effects?","goal":"This clarifies whether a plausible entry-blocking strategy can be developed safely for treatment or prevention, without claiming that any formulation is effective.","evidence":"Published pharmacokinetic, biodistribution, toxicology, and clinical safety comparisons, including whether catalytic attenuation changes antiviral activity and systemic effects.","outcomes":["Independent studies show adequate persistence with no clinically important cardiovascular or other systemic toxicity.","Exposure or safety findings reveal liabilities that prevent translation despite entry inhibition."]}

{"question":"Compare published SARS-CoV-2 small-RNA studies with this SARS-CoV observation to determine whether virus-derived small RNAs are reproducibly produced during infection and associated with host-cell phenotypes.","goal":"This advances understanding of SARS-CoV-2–human interactions and could identify mechanistic or biomarker priorities for prevention and treatment research without implying that any small RNA is a validated target.","evidence":"Published SARS-CoV-2 studies with infection-derived small-RNA sequencing, appropriate infected/uninfected and technical controls, independent replication, host-target or phenotype validation, and comparisons across relevant human airway systems or clinical specimens.","outcomes":["Reproducible SARS-CoV-2 small-RNA production during infection, with sequence-specific host effects replicated across relevant systems, would support a conserved or analogous functional mechanism.","Failure to reproduce infection-associated abundance or effects, or strong dependence on transfection and cell line, would support an artifact or context-limited explanation."]}
{"question":"Compare published SARS-CoV-2 diagnostic and prognostic studies with the source's proposed pathogen-marker implication to assess whether virus-derived small-RNA measurements add validated information beyond viral load and standard clinical variables.","goal":"This advances prevention and clinical research by testing whether small-RNA measurements can improve detection or risk stratification, without claiming clinical utility before independent validation.","evidence":"Published clinical or human-sample studies reporting assay reproducibility, appropriate controls, independent cohorts, comparison with viral load and clinical covariates, and prespecified diagnostic or prognostic performance.","outcomes":["Independent cohorts showing incremental diagnostic or prognostic value after adjustment for viral load and clinical factors would support marker prioritization.","Poor reproducibility, strong technical dependence, or no incremental value would support treating the signal as exploratory rather than clinically actionable."]}
{"question":"Compare published SARS-CoV-2 functional-entry and pregnancy studies with this withdrawn ACE2-expression analysis to determine whether ACE2-positive maternal-fetal or fetal cell populations support reproducible viral entry or infection.","goal":"This advances understanding of SARS-CoV-2 interaction with human tissues and informs prevention research by distinguishing anatomical possibility from validated infection biology, without claiming any intervention is proven.","evidence":"Published SARS-CoV-2 studies using human-relevant placental, fetal, neonatal, or airway systems and reporting ACE2/TMPRSS2 or related cofactors, protein localization, functional entry or infection endpoints, and appropriate negative or perturbation controls.","outcomes":["Reproducible infection or entry in ACE2-positive tissues, with dependence on ACE2 and relevant cofactors, would support a functional tissue-susceptibility model.","Little or no infection despite ACE2 transcripts, or strong dependence on developmental and cofactor context, would support the limitation of expression-only inference."]}
{"question":"Compare controlled clinical studies of maternal SARS-CoV-2 infection with the source's transcriptomic predictions to assess whether vertical transmission or neonatal infection is reproducibly associated with gestational stage, placental ACE2 distribution, or maternal disease context.","goal":"This advances prevention and maternal-fetal disease research by identifying which developmental or clinical contexts warrant focused surveillance, without asserting that transmission or a preventive intervention has been established.","evidence":"Published cohorts with well-timed maternal, placental, amniotic-fluid, cord-blood, neonatal, and postnatal samples; validated viral testing; exposure timing; gestational age; maternal severity; and contamination or alternative-exposure controls.","outcomes":["Consistent detection of virus or serologic evidence in appropriately timed fetal compartments, with controls excluding peripartum exposure, would support true vertical transmission in defined contexts","Repeated negative fetal-compartment testing despite placental ACE2 expression, or positives only after delivery, would support limited intrauterine transmission or an alternative-exposure explanation."]}

{"question":"Do published SARS-CoV-2 studies validate genetically modified primary human airway epithelial cultures as predictive models of virus–host interaction?","goal":"Answering this advances understanding of SARS-CoV-2 interaction with human airway tissue and improves preclinical interpretation without claiming that any treatment or vaccine is proven.","evidence":"Published SARS-CoV-2 studies using differentiated primary human airway cultures with appropriate unmodified controls, replication and innate-response readouts, donor comparisons, and comparison with organoid or patient-derived evidence where available.","outcomes":["Independent studies reproduce SARS-CoV-2 replication and host-response findings across donors and model formats, with concordance to human respiratory observations.","Findings vary substantially by donor or culture state, or fail to correspond to patient-derived evidence, limiting predictive use."]}
{"question":"Do independent published studies externally or prospectively validate CT-based COVID-19 classifiers against appropriate reference standards?","goal":"This advances understanding of COVID-19 diagnosis and can guide prevention and treatment pathways by determining whether imaging assistance is reliable, without claiming that the model improves outcomes.","evidence":"Independent multi-site or prospective studies reporting patient-level splits, external test cohorts, reference-standard definitions, spectrum and prevalence, scanner/site diversity, and clinically relevant error analyses.","outcomes":["External cohorts reproduce sensitivity and specificity across sites and scanners.","Performance falls materially outside the development distribution or differs by disease severity and comparator pneumonia."]}
{"question":"Do published comparisons show that CT adds diagnostic value beyond RT-PCR and clinical assessment in early or atypical COVID-19?","goal":"This clarifies how imaging can support diagnosis and isolation decisions, thereby informing prevention and treatment workflows without treating CT findings as proof of infection or transmission.","evidence":"Published head-to-head or incremental-value studies reporting timing, symptoms, RT-PCR reference methods, alternative pneumonias, prevalence, false-positive and false-negative consequences, and patient-management outcomes.","outcomes":["CT adds reproducible diagnostic or management value beyond RT-PCR and clinical assessment.","CT mainly reflects nonspecific pneumonia patterns and produces clinically important false positives or adds little incremental value."]}

{"question":"Do independent published studies confirm ACE2-based inhibition against authentic SARS-CoV-2 and show activity that translates beyond entry assays?","goal":"This advances understanding of SARS-CoV-2 entry and evaluates a possible treatment or prevention direction without claiming efficacy is proven.","evidence":"Published comparisons reporting authentic-virus or validated translational assays, controls, construct properties, exposure-response interpretation, and limitations.","outcomes":["Independent studies reproduce inhibition against authentic SARS-CoV-2 in human-relevant systems.","Activity is substantially weaker outside the reported in-vitro assays or does not translate to antiviral benefit."]}
{"question":"Do published translational or clinical studies establish whether ACE2-based fusion proteins achieve adequate exposure without unacceptable effects and improve patient-relevant outcomes?","goal":"This distinguishes a mechanistically plausible candidate from a useful treatment or preventive intervention and directly informs safe COVID-19 research.","evidence":"Published pharmacology, safety, and clinical or validated translational comparisons reporting exposure, distribution, adverse events, relevant endpoints, and uncertainty.","outcomes":["Studies show reproducible exposure, acceptable safety, and patient-relevant benefit.","Studies identify inadequate exposure, toxicity, or no benefit despite biochemical or entry inhibition."]}
{"question":"Do independent published studies reproduce SARS-CoV-2-relevant antiviral activity for IVT mRNA-mediated delivery of passive immunotherapy effectors?","goal":"Answering this advances understanding of SARS-CoV-2 interaction with human systems and evaluates a possible treatment or prevention direction without claiming that the intervention is proven.","evidence":"Published SARS-CoV-2 studies comparing IVT mRNA-mediated effector delivery with appropriate controls and reporting expression, antiviral or neutralization endpoints, model relevance, timing, and limitations.","outcomes":["Independent studies reproduce antiviral activity against SARS-CoV-2 or validated human-relevant surrogate systems with consistent effectors and exposure interpretation.","Activity is weak, transient, delivery-limited, or absent in more physiologic systems despite platform efficacy in unspecified models."]}
{"question":"Do published translational or clinical studies show that IVT mRNA delivery provides acceptable safety and adequate exposure for COVID-19 passive immunotherapy?","goal":"Answering this clarifies whether a mechanistically plausible delivery platform can safely support COVID-19 prevention or treatment research, without treating theoretical advantages as demonstrated clinical benefit.","evidence":"Published pharmacology, safety, immunogenicity, and clinical or validated translational comparisons reporting exposure, duration, adverse events, effectors delivered, and patient-relevant endpoints.","outcomes":["Studies demonstrate reproducible exposure and duration with acceptable safety and evidence of antiviral or clinical benefit.","Studies show inadequate exposure, significant tolerability limitations, immune complications, or no patient-relevant benefit despite transient protein expression."]}
{"question":"Do independent published studies validate antiviral activity of Prulifloxacin, Bictegravir, Nelfinavir, or Tegobuvir against SARS-CoV-2 in biochemical, cell-based, or other human-relevant systems?","goal":"This would clarify whether a protease-targeted direction advances COVID-19 treatment research without claiming that any candidate is effective.","evidence":"Published independent studies reporting assay type, authentic-virus or validated surrogate relevance, concentration-response results, controls, cytotoxicity, replication endpoints, and uncertainty.","outcomes":["Independent studies reproduce protease inhibition and antiviral activity with acceptable selectivity.","Candidates show no reproducible antiviral effect, or apparent activity disappears after cytotoxicity and assay-artifact controls."]}
{"question":"Do published translational or clinical comparisons show adequate exposure, tolerability, and patient-relevant benefit for any of the nominated compounds in COVID-19?","goal":"This comparison would separate a mechanistically plausible repurposing hypothesis from a clinically useful intervention and guide safer treatment research.","evidence":"Published pharmacokinetic, safety, target-engagement, randomized or well-controlled clinical evidence, with appropriate comparators and patient-relevant endpoints.","outcomes":["Studies demonstrate relevant exposure, acceptable tolerability, target engagement, and clinical benefit.","Studies show inadequate exposure, toxicity, absent target engagement, or no patient-relevant benefit."]}

{"question":"Compare published SARS-CoV-2 gastrointestinal studies with this colonocyte ACE2 transcriptomic observation to determine whether ACE2-positive intestinal tissue shows reproducible functional infection or clinically relevant disease associations.","goal":"This advances understanding of how SARS-CoV-2 interacts with human tissues and may clarify prevention or treatment targets without treating ACE2 expression as proof of susceptibility or therapeutic efficacy.","evidence":"Published SARS-CoV-2 human intestinal tissue, organoid, ex vivo, or clinical studies reporting validated infection or entry endpoints, ACE2 and relevant cofactors, tissue condition, appropriate controls, and gastrointestinal clinical correlates.","outcomes":["Concordant functional infection or entry associated with ACE2-positive intestinal cells across independent studies would support a gastrointestinal susceptibility model.","Absent or weak infection despite ACE2 expression, or strong dependence on tissue state and cofactors, would support the limitation of expression-only inference."]}
{"question":"Compare published SARS-CoV-2-specific evidence for rivaroxaban, valsartan, and dipyridamole with the source's repurposing suggestions.","goal":"This advances treatment research by distinguishing mechanistic plausibility from selective antiviral activity and patient benefit, without claiming any drug is proven for COVID-19.","evidence":"Published SARS-CoV-2 biochemical, infected-cell, animal, or clinical studies with appropriate controls, clinically relevant exposure context, safety outcomes, and independent replication; observational studies should address confounding by indication.","outcomes":["Reproducible SARS-CoV-2-specific benefit with acceptable safety and appropriate adjustment for disease severity would support further prioritization.","No selective antiviral effect, null controlled clinical outcomes, or safety concerns would support treating these as unvalidated repurposing hypotheses."]}
{"question":"Compare published SARS-CoV-2 and related-coronavirus surveillance studies with the SADS-CoV report to assess whether reservoir distribution, geographic proximity, and ecological overlap reproducibly identify spillover-risk contexts.","goal":"This advances understanding of SARS-CoV-2 emergence and prevention by identifying evidence-supported surveillance contexts, without claiming that any reservoir signal or intervention prevents infection.","evidence":"Published comparative surveillance and outbreak studies reporting host species, sampling geography and intensity, viral relatedness, human or animal exposure pathways, confirmed spillover outcomes, and controls for ascertainment and contact opportunity.","outcomes":["Replication of reservoir and ecological predictors across independent SARS-CoV-2-related surveillance datasets would support their use in risk prioritization.","Loss of associations after adjustment for sampling effort, geography, and contact opportunity would support ascertainment or exposure structure as the main explanation."]}
{"question":"Compare published functional and epidemiological studies of related coronaviruses to determine whether sequence identity predicts host range, human cell entry, or documented spillover.","goal":"This advances treatment and prevention research by clarifying which viral features warrant further surveillance or vaccine-antigen evaluation, without treating genetic similarity as proof of human protection or therapeutic relevance.","evidence":"Published studies pairing comparative genome or spike analyses with validated host-range, entry, replication, animal or human epidemiological outcomes, including negative or discordant examples and appropriate controls.","outcomes":["Consistent prediction of host range or spillover across independent virus panels would support sequence-informed prioritization.","Frequent discordance between sequence similarity and functional or epidemiological outcomes would support requiring phenotypic and exposure evidence before inferring human risk."]}
{"question":"Compare published SARS-CoV-2-specific quercetin evidence with this Zika protease observation.","goal":"This advances treatment research by determining whether the biochemical lead has SARS-CoV-2-relevant support, without claiming that quercetin is effective.","evidence":"Published SARS-CoV-2-specific biochemical and infected-cell studies with appropriate negative and cytotoxicity controls, exposure context, independent replication, and, if available, carefully interpreted controlled clinical outcomes.","outcomes":["Reproducible selective SARS-CoV-2 inhibition at clinically plausible exposure would support further prioritization.","Failure to inhibit SARS-CoV-2, loss of effect after toxicity or assay-interference controls, or dependence on nonphysiologic exposure would support treating the Zika result as non-transferable."]}

{"question":"Compare published SARS-CoV-2-specific studies of cardiac glycosides and other prioritized MERS-CoV screening hits with the supplied MERS-CoV observations.","goal":"This advances treatment research by distinguishing conserved coronavirus activity from MERS-specific or cell-system-specific effects without claiming any treatment is proven.","evidence":"Published SARS-CoV-2 biochemical, infected-cell, animal, and controlled clinical evidence, including cytotoxicity controls, relevant human-cell contexts, exposure and pharmacokinetic information, mechanism confirmation, and independent replication.","outcomes":["Reproducible, selective SARS-CoV-2 inhibition in independent human-relevant systems at clinically plausible exposure would support further prioritization.","Failure to reproduce inhibition, disappearance after toxicity or assay-interference controls, or unacceptable clinical safety would support treating the MERS result as non-transferable."]}
{"question":"Compare the source's Vero-cell therapeutic-index and time-of-addition findings with SARS-CoV-2 studies that use multiple human-relevant systems and clinical endpoints.","goal":"This improves treatment prioritization by testing selectivity, mechanism, and translational relevance rather than relying on screening rank alone.","evidence":"Independent SARS-CoV-2 replication assays with orthogonal readouts, toxicity and interference controls, human-relevant cell or tissue models, pharmacologic exposure comparisons, and well-controlled clinical evidence where available.","outcomes":["Concordant activity across independent SARS-CoV-2 systems with preserved viability and plausible exposure would support translational follow-up.","Discordant system-specific activity, toxicity-linked effects, or null controlled clinical outcomes would weaken treatment prioritization."]}
